// The other half of `static_trait_method_type_only.noe`: a trait's declared-`static` method reached
// on a **value of a concrete type** is E0047.
//
// It is the same judgment the language already makes about an inherent static function, and for the
// same stated reason — the receiver would be evaluated and then discarded. What changed is that a
// *declared* `static` now earns it too, so the modifier means one thing rather than two: it used to
// promise `T.m(…)` under a bound while leaving `x.m(…)` standing, which made the built-in `From`
// (type-only since before the modifier existed) look like a special case of nothing.
//
// All four ways in are pinned, because a rule that holds for three of them is not a rule:
//   * an in-body `impl Trait { … }` block's method;
//   * the standalone `impl Trait for T { … }` spelling of the same thing;
//   * a hoisted DEFAULT the implementor never wrote out;
//   * a bound method HANDLE (`x.m`), which has no receiver to capture either.
//
// The diagnostic names the trait and points at the `static fn` that made the method type-only —
// which is the whole difference from an inherent static function, whose self-less body is right
// there in front of the reader.
// expect: error E0047 at 37:6
// expect: error E0047 at 38:6
// expect: error E0047 at 39:6
// expect: error E0047 at 40:5
// expect: exit 1
trait Codec {
    static fn tag(): string
    static fn kind(): string { return "codec" }
}

struct InBody {
    v: string
    impl Codec { pub fn tag(): string { return "in-body" } }
}

struct Apart { v: string }
impl Codec for Apart { pub fn tag(): string { return "apart" } }

echo InBody { v: "x" }.tag()
echo Apart { v: "x" }.tag()
echo InBody { v: "x" }.kind()
h = InBody { v: "x" }.tag
echo h()
